    "done_when": "Measured by run r-20261002T053133584643-producer-cpu-before-and-after-incremental-reads (report producer-cpu.md and driver.py in /home/ITER/mcintos/.config/reckon/crew/runs/r-20261002T053133584643-producer-cpu-before-and-after-incremental-reads). Over nine growing runs, the producer at current main reads about 87 times the bytes appended. In a window with nothing appended it still reads about 780 MB, and its CPU matches the pre-§11 revision (25.2 vs 25.35 percent). Its live registration carries no bytes_parsed_last_poll. The suspected cause is reckon/crew/recovery.py _admission_refusal (around line 3384): classify_pointer calls it on every poll for every live run, and it scans each stream to EOF with no cursor and no stat gate. The node confirms or refutes that, and removes every whole-stream read from the poll path, by reading through the producer's per-run cursor or skipping a run whose stream is unchanged since the last poll. The live producer then records bytes_parsed_last_poll in its registration. tests/test_producer_reads_only_what_changed.py gains a case: a poll over unchanged runs reads zero stream bytes on the admission path as well. The node re-runs the driver's after arm at its head on the fleet allocation and states the steady-window read bytes, the growth-window read/appended ratio and mean CPU beside the measure's before figures. The declared negative control is executed and logged. Base and head arms of tests/test_producer_reads_only_what_changed.py, tests/test_pointer_classification_is_memoised.py and tests/test_backend_observation.py show zero added failures.",
    "estimated_hours": 1.0,
    "goal": "The live producer's per-poll stream reads scale with the bytes appended, not with total stream size",
